# Movement brief 003 — Iron Skin

- PWA book id: fractured-path/book-02-iron-circuit (Monroe 1.3 edition)
- Series id: fractured-path
- Author: Opus (Claude Opus 5.5)
- Chapters: 16–22 (7 chapters) → `manuscript/chapter-16.md` … `chapter-22.md`
- Word budget: ~37,000
- Owner names: existing canon names only (Brom, Dace, Vell, Lira, Wendel, Corrin, Coss, Havel). No new names.
- Read before drafting: source chapters 8, 9 and 10; the end of Movement 2; `BOOK_MAP.md` §3 B3/B5, §5 (Brom and Coss cutaways),
  §6.1, §8 items 22 and 24, §11 a, g, k, r; `STATE_LEDGER.md` (latest append); `craft/NAME_REGISTRY.md` collision #6 and the Book 2 rule
  on "Iron Skin" spelled in full; Book 1 `chapter-24.md` (Coss's flag scene, for his voice).

## Where we enter

Three weeks after a large stranger told Cael he was writing the wrong things and left. The notebook now records architecture. Lira is in
the middle of her deliberate losing season and getting visibly better. Havel's compliant report has gone up the chain with one marker in
it that nobody placed.

## What this movement is for

Brom arrives as a person before he arrives as an opponent — and as the first problem Cael's method cannot read. The first third of the
book closes here: the system is fully taught by the end of this movement, and the reader should be able to keep a character sheet.

Consequential turns and promises:
- **Brom cutaway (~5,000), opening the movement:** sixteen; Velmere's three generations of Gold; Iron Skin at Copper as an event to be
  managed; the corrective instructor and the plan to manufacture a better reading; leaving by the unlocked gate; the river city ("purses
  honest, records loose"), learning to be hit by people who meant it; the redirect invented as an economy measure and then discovered to
  return force; the practitioner network's verdict ("the keeper's ledger is worth more than a registry entry"); four hundred miles in
  stages; reading Vell's conservative ratings; three weeks of watching Cael; the pressure-read's library and Cael's signature that comes
  back *wrong* — flickers of Wind, of Pressure, none wearing the shape, orbiting a center he can't find. Brom asks Dace. Body training from
  twelve, Iron Skin and its read since his Kindling at fourteen (BOOK_MAP §11 g).
- **The introduction** verbatim (BOOK_MAP §8 item 22), then Brom's addendum that Cael listened well.
- **Coss cutaway (~2,500):** Havel's compliant report reaches the supervising contact of record. Coss sees the marker beside the flag he
  could not read in Book 1, recognizes the same current above his clearance, and chooses — for the better part of a year, as canon says —
  not to ask. Nothing is written. No contact with Cael.
- **Two weeks of preparation:** the reading rooms (four pages on who owns Iron Skin, one sentence on what it does); Corrin's "punching
  water that decided halfway through to be a wall"; watching twelve sessions; the method fails on a subject who initiates nothing; notation
  rebuilt as *stimulus / latency / response*; latency shortening with familiarity; the inhale-hold; sixty-percent heel load; the coverage
  map and the gap below the knee; the static-target reset gap and its live widening; "what does the hardening cost him?"
- **Cael's own inventory, honestly:** Wind-adjacent is insurance against a man who doesn't commit; Pressure-adjacent's delivery face is
  worse than useless against a surface that returns force. Baseline: chain-of-two with pre-loaded breath works; chain-of-three confirmed
  unavailable at a cost. Lira drills him without fragments: "Leave it home. You can't half-carry a thing into a fight."
- **One interim bout on the page** (~1,800): a read-fed burst costs less than a reactive one. Then the drill that proves it, and the
  ceiling of three amplified strikes logged on the near side of the line.
- **Lira vs Wendel** (~2,500): technique superiority, the off-side priming lag she found herself; Dace's question; "Yes." Vell's entry
  recommending provisional Iron-equivalent pending two confirming bouts, with the line about her own delay — close to an apology.
- **Brom and Cael without a fight between them:** "She's going to hit Silver-tier before she's done." Why Cael does any of this ("to
  understand what I am before anyone else gets to decide it for me"); Lira's correction on the step ("you're not building it by
  yourself"); "What do you see when you use Iron Skin's pressure read?" — "Something I've never seen before." — the shelf that isn't there.

Physical challenges: the interim bout; Lira vs Wendel; Lira's fragmentless drilling; the baseline chain test.

Development: Brom's arc starts in earnest (four to five beats); Lira (two to three); Vell (one); Dace (one); Coss (one to two). Cael
≈4–5 per 10k. The first third ends inside this movement: keep progression vocabulary high through chapter 20, then let it begin to recede.

## What must remain true / withheld

- The Brom bout does not happen in this movement; end before it (about four days out).
- Brom's read of Cael is absence of a category, never a diagnosis. Nobody suggests Cael takes abilities by watching (Book 3).
- Coss's cutaway names no origin and makes no note; he files nothing until Movement 5.
- Lira is *provisional* Iron-equivalent at most; confirmation bouts come later.
- Velmere stays as written (owner's call). Brom's family does not write to him in this book.
- Bronze-equivalent is the top of the circuit ladder (§11 k). Reader Standard.

## What the author owns

How the Brom cutaway is braided into Cael's chapters; how many of the twelve sessions are dramatized; the reading-room texture; which
interim bout is shown; Lira's fight tactics; the boarding-house evenings. Humor: Brom's economy of speech meeting Lira's. The author may
let Cael and Brom argue over breakfast about latency once they are speaking.

## Where we leave pressure

A scheduled bout against a friend-in-waiting whose Path is a machine for returning force, with a hypothesis (a transition window in the
third exchange) built from two weeks of honest watching — and a plan that locks one of Cael's two fragments away by rule. The main floor
is posted. The district is betting against him at unflattering numbers.

