# Movement 003 editorial review — chapters 16–22

## Review status and coverage

This is a fresh-context simulated editorial read with canon evidence, not a blind cold read and not a real human audience test. Coverage is the complete supplied movement: chapters 16–22, seven chapters and 35,830 measured prose words. I also read the supplied edition brief, book map, state ledger through Movement 2, canon rules, universe bible, movement packet, numerical formula, and overlap summary. The movement is complete for its declared scope and stops before the Brom bout as required.

## Unscored reader response

Interest caught immediately in Brom reading Vell's ledger from the front. The action is quiet, but the test he applies to the book reveals his values before his history explains them. His unlocked gate, the family that treats him like a kindly managed defect, and the boat-shed losses make him matter quickly. Lira matters almost as much: her Wendel bout is the movement's emotional and physical payoff, and Vell's written admission of error gives that win consequence beyond a rating.

Interest slipped slightly in the middle of the preparation run. The individual discoveries are good, but chapters 19, 21, and 22 each restate versions of the same tactical conclusion—Wind is insurance, Pressure is locked, Brom's hardening has an account, and the third exchange is the test. The Coss cutaway is thematically strong but also delays the immediate bout line after its institutional meaning is already clear.

I expect the Brom hypothesis to fail on contact in a way that teaches Cael something the notebook could not, while turning the opponent into the friend already promised by their honesty. I would continue without hesitation.

## Reader lenses

Scores are editorial judgments, not demographic findings. Confidence refers to confidence in the judgment from this seven-chapter sample.

| Dimension | Fluent thirteen-year-old | Adult genre reader | Exact location and reason | Confidence |
|---|---:|---:|---|---|
| Opening pull | 8 | 9 | Ch16 opening, “The keeper's book was public”: Brom tests a trustworthy ledger and finds the unclassifiable boy. It gives a concrete mystery before the family history. | High |
| Keep reading | 8 | 9 | Ch22 closing plan, “He hasn't lied to the column”: the posted main-floor bout, four-to-one odds, and a trusted hypothesis create strong forward pressure. Repeated plan recaps soften the acceleration slightly. | High |
| Interest / freshness | 9 | 9 | Ch18, “The gap is the subject,” and Ch22, “You're not on one”: studying a defender through latency and an embodied “shelf” is specific to these people and this system. | High |
| Clarity / flow | 7 | 8 | Ch18's mill-wheel explanation and ch20's six-mark drill make difficult mechanics clear on one pass. The ch19/ch21/ch22 tactical repetitions and several long institutional reflections slow flow more than they confuse it. | High |
| Character attachment | 9 | 9 | Ch16, Brom leaving through the unlocked gate; ch22, Lira's “don't sit on my step and tell me *on your own*.” Both characters expose hurt through choices rather than speeches about deserving sympathy. | High |
| Humor / warmth | 9 | 9 | Ch20 at the baker's side door, especially Brom's inability to “look poor,” and ch21's onion-table scene. The comic habits belong separately to Lira, Brom, and the boarding-house family. | High |
| Action / suspense | 9 | 9 | Ch21, Lira vs. Wendel, exchanges three through six: each fighter adapts, the right-side problem remains trackable, fatigue matters, and the finishing catch-and-send is earned. | High |
| Progression payoff | 9 | 9 | Ch19's two Blade-bout bursts and ch20's controlled drill prove that a read burst is cheaper than a reactive one. Lira's Wendel win likewise converts months of watching into capability and status. | High |
| Connection | 9 | 10 | Ch22, Vell's retroactive marks and written fault, pays off her ch16 promise to correct every touched line. The gaze ration, ten-degree blind spot, Power Log costs, Havel's report, and Lira's lamp all carry forward accurately. | High |
| Read-aloud quality | 8 | 9 | Ch21's Wendel fight has clear attribution and physical referents; Brom's clipped speech is distinct by ear. A few layered explanatory paragraphs in ch17–20 ask more sustained attention than the surrounding dialogue and action. | Medium-high |

## Continuity, canon, and story findings

### Result

No verified canon contradiction was found. The movement fulfills the packet's required turns and ends at the correct pressure point. The only substantive editorial finding is pacing through repeated tactical recapitulation, not a state or rule error.

### State and chronology

- Brom's history is consistent with the binding reconciliation: body training begins at twelve; Iron Skin and its read arrive at Kindling at fourteen; he leaves Velmere at fourteen, spends about two years on the circuit, and is therefore sixteen here. Ch16 keeps all of those distinctions clear.
- The challenge in ch17 is “two weeks from today.” Ch22 closes with “Four days,” after roughly ten days of observation and drilling. The bout correctly remains outside this movement.
- Cael remains fifteen, Assessed, and at two partial fragments. There is no acquisition notice or tier advancement. Lira remains Copper-formal and moves only to provisional Iron-equivalent pending two confirming bouts. Brom remains Copper-formal and Iron-equivalent in Vell's circuit ledger.
- Cael's existing physical costs remain coherent: the left-hip line, held breath, landing lock, grey vision on the failed third burst, Pressure's hollow under the ribs, and the old wrist risk all constrain choices. Nothing becomes free because it is convenient.

### Power rules and knowledge boundaries

- The chain of two requires a visible preload; the attempted third fails at a real cost. The movement does not silently expand the Wind-adjacent deployment range.
- The informed/reactive burst distinction is demonstrated twice—first under live pressure in ch19, then under controlled conditions in ch20—before Cael rules it true. This is good progression causality.
- Pressure's delivery face remains capped at three working uses and is locked away against Brom for a stated mechanical reason. Cael does not test the fourth past the safe side of the boundary.
- Brom reads Cael as uncategorizable—“a shelf that isn't there”—not as someone who integrates witnessed abilities. Cael withholds the fragments and Power Log. The Book 3 mechanism is not disclosed early.
- Coss sees an unsigned, above-grade marker and chooses not to query it. He does not learn its origin, the Level 4 language, the registry sub-layer, or any intention behind it, and he writes no note. This respects both the movement brief and the reveal schedule.
- The movement establishes Iron Skin through witnessed behavior, hearsay, and limited books. It does not grant Cael privileged guild knowledge.

### Relationships and supporting arcs

- Brom's honesty is active rather than merely asserted: he declines to poison the latency data, pays newcomers, pays Lira's partner again, answers only to the edge of what he has actually observed, and lets Cael study him at a cost to his own bout advantage.
- Lira's Wendel win is independent of Cael's page. Her choice to ask for the provisional line, and Cael's refusal to supply a facial answer, preserve her agency.
- Vell's admission—“The fault is the keeper's”—is a strong integrity beat and directly pays off the standard she states to Brom in ch16.
- Dace, Keth, the heavyset man, Coss, and Wendel each make a consequential choice of their own. The supporting cast does not function only as an audience for Cael.

### Plan adherence, not canon error

- This movement is deliberately cutaway-heavy: Brom owns most of ch16 plus a ch20 scene, and Coss owns the final two scenes of ch17. That is consistent with the packet's requested Brom and Coss allocations. The 87/13 POV ratio is a book-level target and cannot be passed or failed from this movement alone.
- The packet asks progression teaching to remain high through ch20 and begin receding afterward. The manuscript follows that broad shape: ch18–20 teach hard; ch21–22 move toward the Wendel payoff, friendship, honest records, and the posted bout, though the final written plan briefly raises the mechanical density again.

## Reader Standard

Pass. The movement is suitable for the stated thirteen-year-old reader standard:

- No profanity, obscenity, crude slang, blasphemy, sexual content, or innuendo appears.
- Violence is trackable and consequential without gore. Bruises, damaged wrists, breath loss, fatigue, fear, and recovery all matter.
- Honesty, restraint, courage, loyalty, fair measurement, care for weaker trainees, and admitting fault carry real costs and receive narrative weight. Wrong institutional habits are named as wrong; cruelty is not rewarded.
- The technical language is taught through concrete images—the mill slack, the purse/account, the bow returning to straight, the shelf—and is generally accessible on one read.

